# Soil Health & Hydrology Knowledge Guide

## 1. Soil Moisture Volumetric Water Content (VWC) Thresholds

| Crop Type | Permanent Wilting Point (%) | Water Stress Trigger (%) | Field Capacity (%) | Saturation / Over-Irrigation (%) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grapes (Clay Loam) | < 16% | < 26% | 34% - 38% | > 42% |
| Pomegranate (Medium Black) | < 14% | < 24% | 32% - 36% | > 40% |
| Sugarcane (Heavy Clay) | < 18% | < 28% | 38% - 44% | > 48% |
| Banana (Silty Clay) | < 17% | < 27% | 36% - 42% | > 46% |
| Wheat (Loam) | < 12% | < 22% | 28% - 34% | > 38% |

### Interpretation Rules
- **Under-Irrigation Risk**: VWC below the Water Stress Trigger for > 48 hours causes stomatal closure, leaf wilting, suppressed fruit expansion, and steep NDVI decline.
- **Over-Irrigation Risk**: VWC exceeding Saturation limit for > 72 hours leads to soil root hypoxia (oxygen starvation), root rot pathogens (Pythium, Phytophthora), and impaired nutrient uptake.

## 2. Soil pH and Nutrient Availability
- **Optimal Range**: 6.0 to 7.5 for most horticultural crops.
- **Low pH (< 5.5)**: Increases Aluminum and Manganese toxicity; reduces Phosphorus, Calcium, and Magnesium availability.
- **High pH (> 8.0)**: Induces Iron, Zinc, and Manganese deficiencies (interveinal chlorosis).

## 3. Soil Electrical Conductivity (EC) Salinity Rules
- **< 1.5 dS/m**: Non-saline, ideal for sensitive horticultural crops (Grapes, Pomegranate).
- **1.5 – 3.0 dS/m**: Moderate salinity; minor yield reduction in sensitive crops.
- **> 4.0 dS/m**: Severe salinity stress; root water absorption impaired due to osmotic pressure.
